Channel recovery, NVK's half. NullMoth's own change (not an upstream Mesa commit).
A GPU fault on one channel, or a channel that makes no progress for NVRM_FLUSH_TIMEOUT_MS, used to end the whole
Vulkan device, and with it the Metal device built on it. RM tears down only the faulting channel group, so this patch
builds a new channel beside the old one, frees the old one, and moves the new one behind the same context: the same
semaphore page and command ring, the channel-wide state sent again from host copies, the flushes that had not started
replayed, and the one that was running dropped (the CPU stores its sequence number into the semaphore; its binary
fences answer "did not run" through nvmtl_nvk_fence_dropped_v1). Before that, a stalled wait rings the doorbell again
at half the budget. A fault inside replayed work drops everything in flight. Only channel exceptions and stalls are
rebuilt, at most 3 times in 30 s per channel; every other loss ends exactly as 2.9 ended, plus one line naming it.
A system sleep is not a fault: while the kext reports the GPU suspended for a sleep or wake, a stalled wait looks
again instead of rebuilding (for at most 360 s of awake time per sleep), and neither the thread that submits to a
channel nor the doorbell ring makes an RM call or escape while it holds the channel's mutex (the boost lease is
renewed before it, deferred frees are reaped after it); only the recovery sequence (and the armed test hook) calls RM
with it held, and for a channel that merely stands still it starts only once the kext no longer reports the GPU
suspended or those 360 s have passed.
The pure part is nvkmd/nvrm/nvkmd_nvrm_recover.h, with its host test nvkmd_nvrm_recover_test.c beside it.
nvkmd/nvrm/nvrm_chan_note_abi.h is a byte-for-byte copy of kexts/NVRM/nvrm_chan_note_abi.h (escape 0xE8: counters in
the kext and the adapter's power state; a kext without it fails the escape once and is not asked again).
NVK_CHANNEL_RECOVER=0 runs the 2.9 code; NVK_FAULT_INJECT=<stall|fake|reset|mmu>:<n>[:<process>] is the test hook.
Both are read from the environment or from /Library/GPUBundles/nvmtl/nvrm610.conf; a release build sees only the file.
tools/test_channel_recover.py holds the wiring.
A fault on any copy engine is a channel loss that is rebuilt: the upload channel of an RTX 50 card runs on copy
engine 4, whose fault is Xid 71, and 2.9 lost the device on it. A channel that only copies replays all its work,
the flush that was running included, and a fault inside that replayed work ends the device.
The patch also changes 2.9's nvrm610.conf reader (src/nouveau/vulkan/nvmtl_cfg.h): it keeps the last line that
begins with a name, as the Metal plugin's reader does, where 2.9 kept the first, so a shipped line or a comment
above the user's own line does not win in a process that loads NVK without the plugin; the header's comment lists
where it still differs from the plugin's reader. The three file-local copies of 2.9's reader (nvkmd_nvrm_ctx.c,
nvk_physical_device.c, nvk_cmd_dispatch.c, for NVRM_ASYNC, NVK_DIAG, NVK_QUEUE_COUNT and NVMTL_GDQ_DQ) are removed
and those four names are read through it too.
Leaving this patch out of the chain also brings back the first-line-wins reader.

