+/* nvkmd_nvrm_recover.c - rebuilding a faulted or stalled channel in place, instead of losing the Vulkan device.
+ *
+ * The decisions are in nvkmd_nvrm_recover.h (pure, host-tested). This file supplies what they need: the per-channel
+ * locks, the RM calls that build a new channel and free the old one, the swap of the new channel into the context,
+ * the doorbell, the kext's channel note (nvrm_chan_note_abi.h), the statistics, and the test hook.
+ *
+ * The ladder, on a channel a wait is blocked on:
+ *   1. half the stall budget without progress: write GPPut again and ring the doorbell (nvkmd_nvrm_slot_kick);
+ *   2. a recoverable fault, or the whole budget without progress: build a new channel beside the old one, free the old
+ *      one, give the new one the channel-wide state and the flushes that had not started, and drop the flush that was
+ *      running (the CPU stores its sequence number into the semaphore), nvrm_rec_run;
+ *   3. a fault inside work an earlier rebuild replayed: the same, dropping everything that was in flight;
+ *   4. anything else (an unrecoverable kind, the budget of 3 rebuilds in 30 s spent, an RM refusal): the 2.9 result,
+ *      VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST with the 2.9 lines, and one line that names the kind of loss and why it was not rebuilt.
+ *
+ * NVK_CHANNEL_RECOVER=0 (environment, or a line in /Library/GPUBundles/nvmtl/nvrm610.conf) turns all of it off: the
+ * hooks then run the 2.9 code unchanged. NVK_FAULT_INJECT=<stall|fake|reset|mmu>:<n>[:<process name>] is the test
+ * hook (nvrm_rec_inject_for_flush). A healthy GPU prints nothing from this file. */
